remove a symlink without chmod in the temp cleanup

0005 reaches the loader through a symbolic link named after the program
being executed, in a private temporary directory
("$PROOT_TMP_DIR/exec-<pid>-XXXXXX"), because AT_EXECFN is the path as
*given* to execve(2).  When that directory is removed, clean_temp_cwd()
(src/proot/src/path/temp.c) chmods every entry 0700 before unlinking it
-- and chmod(2) follows a symbolic link.  The link's target is
PROOT_LOADER: a file the platform allows to be execve(2)'d, which is
not the same as a file the app may chmod(2).  On Android the loader
lives in the native library directory, where execve(2) is permitted and
chmod(2) is not, so every launch of every program printed five lines:

    proot warning: cant chmod 'bash': Permission denied
    proot warning: cant chmod 'run-parts': Permission denied
    proot warning: cant chmod 'locale-check': Permission denied
    proot warning: cant chmod 'whoami': Permission denied
    proot error: cant remove '/data/data/dev.eclipse.ssh/files/linux/tmp/exec-8254-bervFx': Directory not empty

The `continue` on the failed chmod skips the unlink() below it, so the
directory is left still holding the links and the rmdir(2) that follows
fails ENOTEMPTY.  The four warnings are the four programs whose links
the session had made; the leak is one directory per proot process --
every session, every setup step, every health probe -- and nothing ever
removes it, because it is named after a pid that will not run again.

The rule the fix restores is the plain one: a failed chmod is not a
reason to skip an unlink.  A symbolic link has no mode of its own on
Linux -- chmod(2) follows it, so what it would change is the file the
link names, which for a loader link is a file this process was never
allowed to change.  unlink(2) asks the containing directory for
permission instead, and that directory is this process' own.  So an
entry that is a link is unlinked as a link, and never chmod'ed.

Links are recognized by d_type where the filesystem fills it in, and
by lstat(2) where it does not (DT_UNKNOWN), so a link on a filesystem
that leaves d_type empty does not leak identically.  The recursion test
is left as `entry->d_type == DT_DIR`, which is already false for
DT_LNK: a link to a directory is unlinked rather than entered.

Evidence, x86_64 Linux, the pinned fork + this patch set, the pinned
amd64 26.04 rootfs.  The reproducing trick is to point PROOT_LOADER at
a root-owned file this user may not chmod (0755 root => EPERM; Android
refuses with EACCES, and either way the call fails and the entry is
skipped).  That is also why 0005's own host evidence never saw this:
the host's libproot-loader.so is owned by the user running proot, so
the chmod succeeds there and nothing leaks.  The loader is therefore
not a working loader in this proof and the guest process dies, which is
the `vpid 1: terminated with signal 11` line and is expected; the lines
that matter are the two below it.

    $ T=/tmp/leak; mkdir -p $T; cd /tmp
    $ PROOT_TMP_DIR=$T PROOT_LOADER=/usr/bin/true proot-before -r rootfs-amd64 -0 /usr/bin/true
    proot info: vpid 1: terminated with signal 11
    proot warning: cant chmod 'true': Operation not permitted
    proot error: cant remove '/tmp/leak/exec-3779996-GypsGV': Directory not empty
    $ find $T -mindepth 1
    /tmp/leak/exec-3779996-GypsGV
    /tmp/leak/exec-3779996-GypsGV/true       # -> /usr/bin/true

    $ PROOT_TMP_DIR=$T PROOT_LOADER=/usr/bin/true proot-after -r rootfs-amd64 -0 /usr/bin/true
    proot info: vpid 1: terminated with signal 11
    $ find $T -mindepth 1        # nothing: neither warning, and no leftover

`before` and `after` are the same pristine extract of the pinned fork
with 0001..0005 applied, differing only by this patch, and are built on
the host with the fork's own GNUmakefile:

    $ cd src/proot/src && make clean
    $ CPPFLAGS="-I/tmp/proothost/work/config \
        -I/tmp/proothost/talloc-2.4.2 \
        -I/tmp/proothost/talloc-2.4.2/lib/replace \
        -I/tmp/proothost/work -I/tmp/proothost/shim" \
      LDFLAGS="-L/tmp/proothost/work" GIT=true make -j2

The include paths are the talloc 2.4.2 source, the config.h and
libtalloc.a built from it (both under /tmp/proothost/work), and a
linux/ashmem.h this glibc host's headers lack.  An android build
passes the same two variables into the same makefile; the flags it
uses are in linux/build.gradle.kts.

What this does not fix, and should not be read as fixing: a proot that
is SIGKILLed still leaves its whole temp directory behind, because the
directory is removed by talloc destructors and a killed process runs
none.  The app reclaims those by name.  This patch is about the
directories a proot that exits normally leaves.

Nor does it change the treatment of a *real* directory that a
filesystem reports as DT_UNKNOWN: the recursion test still asks for
DT_DIR, so such a directory falls through to unlink(2), fails EISDIR
and warns.  That is pre-existing upstream behaviour, it is not what
this app hits (links are the only entries an "exec" directory holds),
and it is deliberately out of scope here.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; applied by
fetchLinuxSource in linux/build.gradle.kts (stacks on 0005).
--- a/src/proot/src/path/temp.c
+++ b/src/proot/src/path/temp.c
@@ -96,6 +96,7 @@
 
 	while (1) {
 		struct dirent *entry;
+		int is_link;
 
 		errno = 0;
 		entry = readdir(dir);
@@ -106,11 +107,37 @@
 		    || strcmp(entry->d_name, "..") == 0)
 			continue;
 
-		status = chmod(entry->d_name, 0700);
-		if (status < 0) {
-			note(NULL, WARNING, SYSTEM, "cant chmod '%s'", entry->d_name);
-			nb_errors++;
-			continue;
+		/* A symbolic link's own mode is meaningless on Linux:
+		 * chmod(2) follows the link, so what it would change is
+		 * the file the link names, not the link itself.  For the
+		 * loader links an "exec" directory holds, that file lies
+		 * at a path this process is allowed to execve(2) but not
+		 * to chmod(2), so the call fails and -- before this test
+		 * existed -- the entry was left behind.  unlink(2) asks
+		 * the containing directory for permission instead, and
+		 * this process made that directory.  So a link is
+		 * unlinked and never chmod'ed, and a chmod that would
+		 * fail is no reason to skip the unlink.  */
+		if (entry->d_type == DT_LNK)
+			is_link = 1;
+		else if (entry->d_type == DT_UNKNOWN) {
+			struct stat entry_stat;
+
+			/* A filesystem that does not fill in d_type
+			 * would leak the same way.  */
+			status = lstat(entry->d_name, &entry_stat);
+			is_link = (status == 0 && S_ISLNK(entry_stat.st_mode));
+		}
+		else
+			is_link = 0;
+
+		if (!is_link) {
+			status = chmod(entry->d_name, 0700);
+			if (status < 0) {
+				note(NULL, WARNING, SYSTEM, "cant chmod '%s'", entry->d_name);
+				nb_errors++;
+				continue;
+			}
 		}
 
 		if (entry->d_type == DT_DIR) {
